#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98c98a56-ca57-5dc6-9dc4-c40da53806f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3063" :question/problem "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$ to $t = 2\\pi$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/0c0c2dffe8d85c38943e919e899e3373.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime }(t) &= \\langle \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin 2t)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 3\\cos t, 4\\cos 2t\\rangle  \\\\\n\\Vert f^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert  &= \\sqrt{(3\\cos t)^{2} + (4\\cos 2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 16\\cos^{2} 2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{a}^{b}\\Vert f^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 16\\cos^{2} 2t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e310cbe-9d6b-5bc4-960a-8813333bc8be" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8932c22b-465f-5a48-962c-e53730e5ba40" :question/math-academy-id "e-3064" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf g (t) = \\langle 3 - 2 \\sin 2t, \\: 1 - 2\\cos 2t \\rangle$ from $t=\\dfrac \\pi 3$ to $\\pi$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/bfcb501a54f7ab19db15a4c4726f7f63.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng^{\\prime }(t) &= \\langle \\frac{d}{dt}(3 - 2\\sin 2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1 - 2\\cos 2t)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle  - 4\\cos 2t, 4\\sin 2t\\rangle  \\\\\n\\Vert g^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert  &= \\sqrt{(-4\\cos 2t)^{2} + (4\\sin 2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} 2t + 16\\sin^{2} 2t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{a}^{b}\\Vert g^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4dt \\\\\n&= 4t \\mid _{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\\n&= 4(\\pi  - \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8\\pi }{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "118ad24e-627f-51ff-9b9c-9319c50956f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6303" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/3fca46c2b71edab0ded0e3d361c48c4f.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\langle \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle \\cos t,-\\sin t, 1\\rangle  \\\\\n\\Vert r^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert  &= \\sqrt{(\\cos t)^{2} + (-\\sin t)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{0}^{2\\pi }\\Vert r^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}t \\mid _{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}(2\\pi  - 0) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\\pi \n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c60eb52-e275-5f9e-8b64-8f24c6fbbbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2\\cos^{2} 2t + 3\\sin^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117731} {:answer/id #uuid "5068c443-8395-505c-92d8-71cea0dfc17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4\\cos^{2} 2t + 9\\sin^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117732} {:answer/id #uuid "748e7d72-d682-5524-af39-e923fed4a5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} 2t + 9\\cos^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117733} {:answer/id #uuid "7997a0c6-f7e9-5d98-aedd-32f939d3e168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2\\sin^{2} 2t + 9\\cos^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117734} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb306df-b1bd-5be5-b18b-0285d0ccf69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\cos^{2} 2t + 9\\sin^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5068c443-8395-505c-92d8-71cea0dfc17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4\\cos^{2} 2t + 9\\sin^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117732} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0ba63d-f616-5753-b3ec-5ef064c6cb5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "994d254c-8dfb-50db-8abf-3c167a677836" :question/math-academy-id "q-33728" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/q-33728.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{g} ( t ) = \\langle  \\sin   2 t , \\, \\cos   3 t \\rangle$ from $t = 0$ to $t = \\pi $?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Differentiate to get $\\mathbf g'(t)=\\langle2\\cos(2t),-3\\sin(3t)\\rangle$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{4\\cos^2(2t)+9\\sin^2(3t)}$. Thus $$L=\\int_0^\\pi\\sqrt{4\\cos^2(2t)+9\\sin^2(3t)}\\,dt.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a326f7cd-e00b-54c0-ab9f-590b9be9434f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117749} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb9c90d-0331-573b-b927-332415a3e3e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117750} {:answer/id #uuid "d44f81fe-365e-561c-a75f-c0401199df55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117751} {:answer/id #uuid "6f06dbb2-f486-597f-8823-cbcf836cf795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117752} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc95fd8-7241-5de7-8389-734d1f51aa67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a326f7cd-e00b-54c0-ab9f-590b9be9434f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117749} :answer-field/id #uuid "78456853-af32-5f74-8527-d53770ea5c6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7f25b66-94f4-5ecd-88ef-6b78d26ced87" :question/math-academy-id "q-33779" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{f} ( t ) = \\langle \\cos   t + t \\sin   t , \\, \\sin   t - t \\cos   t , \\, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} {t}^{2} \\rangle$\n\nfrom $t = 1$ to $t = 3$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The product rule gives $\\mathbf f'(t)=\\langle t\\cos t,t\\sin t,\\sqrt3t\\rangle$. For $1\\le t\\le3$, its magnitude is $\\sqrt{t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t+3)}=2t$. Hence $$L=\\int_1^3 2t\\,dt=[t^2]_1^3=9-1=8.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e290ec0-ed23-5528-bc18-4a7062dfb935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{1}^{2}e^{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 2t + 4}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117785} {:answer/id #uuid "f058d6ff-6965-51d2-9b3f-a0cb15d51ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{1}e^{t}(t + 2)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117786} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8950e0-1b63-5027-b1bb-894cd121725f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{1}^{2}e^{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 2t + 5}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117787} {:answer/id #uuid "6e230b78-d713-59b8-ac85-31bb4b8aeede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{1}^{2}e^{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 2t + 6}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117788} {:answer/id #uuid "75a5a4de-3dd1-565b-80f0-e1edaf7b73f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{1}e^{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 2t + 2}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e230b78-d713-59b8-ac85-31bb4b8aeede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{1}^{2}e^{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 2t + 6}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117788} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58ff40c-133f-51e7-b56c-614aadcb292a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bc45ddc-02bd-5a1e-bfa3-f99f90a0c552" :question/math-academy-id "q-33862" :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{f} ( t ) = {e}^{t} ( \\, \\mathbf{i} + 2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + t \\, \\mathbf{k} )$ from $t = 1$ to $t = 2$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf f(t)=\\langle e^t,2e^t,te^t\\rangle$. The product rule gives $\\mathbf f'(t)=e^t\\langle1,2,t+1\\rangle$, so $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|=e^t\\sqrt{1+4+(t+1)^2}$. Therefore $$L=\\int_1^2e^t\\sqrt{t^2+2t+6}\\,dt.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e310cbe-9d6b-5bc4-960a-8813333bc8be" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4bc97f-5826-50a6-b55c-671bc6143002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119200} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9fe507-85ba-53bc-9a19-4653edcc27e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119201} {:answer/id #uuid "52c887e6-0bce-51ce-976a-f47f65eecc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119202} {:answer/id #uuid "594ccbc3-3056-5d25-815a-b927d40a57c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119203} {:answer/id #uuid "761b3321-09b9-5264-a017-77897db324fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf4bc97f-5826-50a6-b55c-671bc6143002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119200} :answer-field/id #uuid "d04d2bfc-a6ff-5c61-844c-9ccfb50f4eb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d67d847a-8cc9-599e-9782-16c3ef20921d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/q-77065.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{g} ( t ) = \\langle  {t}^{3} - 3 t , \\, 3 {t}^{2} \\rangle$ from $t = 1$ to $t = 2$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The velocity is $\\mathbf g'(t)=\\langle3t^2-3,6t\\rangle$. Its speed simplifies to $$\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|=3\\sqrt{(t^2-1)^2+4t^2}=3\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^2}=3(t^2+1).$$ Hence $$L=\\int_1^2 3(t^2+1)\\,dt=[t^3+3t]_1^2=14-4=10.$$"}]}
